export const SAY: Record<string, string> = {"q00":"India's biggest telecom company is raising twenty seven thousand five hundred crore rupees. Not for its network. To repay loans. And its rival Airtel keeps about thirty five rupees more from every customer, every month. So is Jio really the strongest?","q01":"This is our Jio IPO review. Bidding runs from the twenty first to the twenty third of October. Up to twenty seven crore new shares, and no offer for sale, so all the money goes to the company.","q02":"Jio says it holds the industry's largest spectrum, twenty six thousand eight hundred megahertz. Keep that number in mind. Its telecom company had fifty three point three crore customers in June.","q03":"Our question: Jio is the biggest. Is it also the best protected? That's the moat. And is its profit on each rupee rising or falling? That's the margin.","q10":"Chapter one. The IPO.","q11":"Reliance Industries owns sixty six point four three percent of Jio Platforms before the issue. A Meta affiliate owns nine point nine eight percent, and Google seven point seven three.","q12":"The twenty seven crore new shares would be about three percent of the company after the issue, by our arithmetic. Reliance stays firmly in control.","q13":"The money: twenty seven thousand five hundred crore rupees prepays loans of Reliance Jio Infocomm, the telecom subsidiary. General corporate purposes can't exceed a quarter of the gross proceeds.","q14":"And the prospectus puts Jio's net debt at zero point three six times EBITDA. So why repay debt at all? Hold on to that number. We come back to it at the end.","q20":"Chapter two. How Jio got here.","q21":"Jio's telecom company launched 4G with voice over LTE in twenty sixteen. The industry report Jio paid for, for this IPO, says it reached forty two point six seven percent of India's broadband customers within a year.","q22":"Jio Platforms was incorporated in November twenty nineteen. In twenty twenty it sold stakes to investors including a Meta affiliate, Google, Silver Lake, K K R and Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund.","q23":"In the August twenty twenty two 5G auction, Jio was reported to have spent eighty eight thousand and seventy eight crore rupees. It then launched what it calls India's first standalone 5G network.","q24":"In the second quarter of financial year twenty five, Jio and other operators raised tariffs. And on the nineteenth of June twenty twenty six, Jio filed its draft prospectus.","q25":"So the company on offer is a ten year old entrant that now leads on subscribers. But does leading mean protected? To answer that, we need the whole industry.","q30":"Chapter three. The industry.","q31":"TRAI's June twenty twenty six data. Jio, fifty one point seven six crore wireless subscribers, thirty nine point eight one percent. Airtel, forty nine point zero six crore, thirty seven point seven three percent. Vodafone Idea, nineteen point eight eight crore. BSNL, nine point three crore.","q32":"One caution. In financial year twenty six, Airtel started counting machine to machine connections in its total. Seven point seven crore of them in March.","q33":"Take machine connections out for everyone, and in March, Jio had forty one point four percent of mobile connections. Airtel, thirty five point one.","q34":"Two rules shape this industry. Spectrum comes through government auctions, or sharing and trading. And licence fees and spectrum charges cost Jio seven point nine seven percent of its revenue last year.","q35":"Those costs fall on every operator, not just Jio. So the moat question has one real test: Jio against Airtel. Does Jio have an edge Airtel can't copy? Let's start with how Jio makes its money.","q40":"Chapter four. The business model.","q41":"Seventy seven percent of Jio's revenue last year came from prepaid connectivity. All of it is distributed by one company: Reliance Retail.","q42":"The telecom company, R J I L, brought in eighty seven point seven percent of revenue and ninety four point nine percent of EBITDA. The parent's own digital services were about eleven percent of revenue. Jio reports one segment, so their profit isn't shown separately.","q43":"Now follow one hundred rupees of revenue. Twenty six rupees run the network, counting rent booked under other expenses. Eight go to licence and spectrum fees. Four and a half to employees, three to selling, one to access charges, and seven to everything else. Fifty rupees are left as operating profit.","q44":"From those fifty, about eighteen and a half go to depreciation, six to interest and seven to tax, while two rupees of other income, mostly interest, come back in. Net profit: twenty and a half rupees.","q45":"One detail. The EBITDA margin in the prospectus, fifty one point nine percent, includes that other income. Without it: forty nine point six, forty nine point two, and fifty percent over the last three years.","q50":"Chapter five. The rivals.","q51":"Now per customer, per month, last year. Jio's telecom company took in about two hundred and twelve rupees and kept about one hundred and nineteen as EBITDA.","q52":"Airtel's India mobile business took in about two hundred and fifty six rupees and kept about one hundred and fifty four. Airtel says its strategy is winning quality customers and premiumising.","q53":"In the June quarter, Airtel had three crore postpaid customers and a mobile ARPU of two hundred and sixty four rupees. Jio's ARPU was two hundred and fifteen point six. The two are defined differently.","q54":"Vodafone Idea took in about one hundred and ninety one rupees and kept about eighty one. After depreciation it made a loss, and it owes the government about one point four five lakh crore rupees.","q55":"So Jio leads on subscribers. But Airtel keeps about thirty five rupees more EBITDA from every customer, every month. The real question isn't who is bigger. It's who is better protected. Let's test Jio's walls, one at a time.","q60":"Chapter six. The moat test.","q61":"Wall one: cost. Jio's network carried two hundred and forty one billion gigabytes last year. Airtel carried about one hundred and one, Vodafone Idea about twenty nine. Jio's figure includes fixed broadband; Airtel's is mobile only.","q62":"By our arithmetic, Jio spent about two rupees thirty four in operating costs per gigabyte. Airtel's mobile business, about four rupees thirty five.","q63":"Even if home broadband were twenty to forty percent of Jio's traffic, which the prospectus doesn't disclose, Jio would still be twenty four to thirty eight percent cheaper. But it also charges less: about five rupees a gigabyte, against Airtel's eight. Verdict: a real cost lead, not yet a margin lead.","q64":"Wall two: spectrum. Remember twenty six thousand eight hundred megahertz? Twenty two thousand of it is the twenty six gigahertz band, a thousand megahertz in each of twenty two circles. That's eighty two percent.","q65":"The prospectus says that band lets Jio deploy fixed wireless access at high capacity. Airtel holds eighteen thousand megahertz of it too. Leave that band out, and Jio holds only about six percent more spectrum than Airtel.","q66":"What's unique is smaller: Jio is the only operator with seven hundred megahertz in every circle, valid until twenty forty two. The prospectus says it gives deep indoor coverage.","q67":"Two dates to note. Four hundred and forty megahertz of Jio's twenty three hundred megahertz band is valid only until twenty thirty. And its telecom licence runs to October twenty thirty three. Verdict: a partial wall.","q68":"Wall three, and maybe the one that matters most: do Jio's customers stay? Monthly churn last year: Jio one point six seven percent, Airtel two point four three, Vodafone Idea three point nine. Over a year, roughly eighteen percent for Jio, twenty six for Airtel. Jio was lower in each of the last three years. Verdict: Jio's strongest wall.","q69":"Wall four: pricing. The prospectus says that in that tariff increase, ARPU rose, but churn rose too, and customer growth slowed.","q6a":"Net additions fell from four point two five crore to sixty four lakh that year, then recovered to three point six two crore. Jio's ARPU rose about thirteen and a half percent in three quarters. Airtel's rose about sixteen. Verdict: borderline.","q6b":"Wall five: distribution and suppliers. Reliance Retail, a subsidiary of Jio's promoter, distributes all of Jio's prepaid. And of three lakh sixty thousand towers Jio uses, one lakh seventy four thousand belong to Summit Digitel. About ninety eight percent of its fibre comes from Jio Digital Fibre. The telecom company can't terminate either agreement.","q6c":"Selling costs were three point one percent of Jio's revenue. Airtel's commission and distribution, three point two five percent. And the Reliance Retail agreement isn't among the documents listed for inspection. Verdict: wide reach, and a dependency.","q6d":"A moat should show up in returns. Jio's return on capital employed, by its own definition, fell from twelve point eight three percent to ten point seven six. Airtel's, by its definition, nineteen percent.","q6e":"On our own measure, counting spectrum dues and leases as capital, Jio earned nine point seven percent before tax. One fact may explain part of it: Jio added one point three two lakh crore rupees of spectrum to its books in the last two years.","q6f":"So: one strong wall, two partial, one borderline, and a dependency. But one line in the prospectus changes how to read Jio's debt.","q70":"Chapter seven. The other reading.","q71":"The strongest case for Jio. The lowest churn. The most data carried. The lowest cost per gigabyte we can measure. And the only nationwide seven hundred megahertz band. Capex fell from forty eight point eight percent of revenue to twenty three point three in two years.","q72":"EBITDA minus cash capex rose from one thousand four hundred and forty nine crore to forty two thousand and seventy one crore rupees. If returns are low partly because so much spectrum is new on the books, they could rise. That case is serious. The filings don't prove it yet.","q80":"Chapter eight. The verdict.","q81":"Back to the debt. The prospectus shows net debt at zero point three six times EBITDA. Its own note says that leaves out deferred payment liabilities: one lakh four thousand five hundred crore rupees still owed for spectrum from the twenty twenty one, twenty two and twenty four auctions.","q82":"Include them, and Jio's ratio is about one point seven times. Airtel's, which already includes them, is one point three six.","q83":"Moat verdict: narrow. On our MoatOS scorecard Jio scores four point eight seven out of ten. Three separate scoring passes landed between four point seven four and five point zero eight. Its walls: scale, the lowest cost per gigabyte, the lowest churn and exclusive seven hundred megahertz. Its limits: similar spectrum to Airtel outside twenty six gigahertz, less EBITDA per customer, lower returns, and one related distributor.","q84":"Margin verdict: rising slowly, not pulling ahead. EBITDA margin without other income stayed near fifty percent. EBIT margin without other income rose from twenty nine point four to thirty one point four percent. Airtel India's rose faster, from twenty three point seven to thirty point four.","q85":"And the cash left after capex, spectrum dues, leases and interest went from minus seventeen thousand five hundred and ninety three crore rupees to plus twenty thousand three hundred and thirty one crore.","q86":"What we know: Jio's scale, its churn lead, its cost per gigabyte lead, and its leverage once spectrum dues are counted.","q87":"What we don't know: the Reliance Retail terms, how much of Jio's traffic is home broadband, and how the next tariff change plays out.","q88":"What to watch: the next tariff change and Jio's churn after it, ARPU against Airtel every quarter, the monitoring agency's reports on how the twenty seven thousand five hundred crore is used, and the twenty thirty spectrum expiry.","q90":"Every number here is from Jio's prospectus, TRAI, and Airtel's and Vodafone Idea's own reports, or our arithmetic on them. Links are in the description.","q91":"So how does Airtel keep about thirty five rupees more from every customer, with similar spectrum outside twenty six gigahertz? That's our next video: Airtel versus Jio, who really has the moat?","q92":"Subscribe for the filings that matter."};
